#include "curriculum.h"
/* Extended study notes: explanatory detail, pitfalls and practice prompts. */
LessonDetail lessonDetails[24] = {
    {"1. Include a header for functions you call, such as stdio.h for printf.\n2. Program execution enters main; statements inside its braces run in order.\n3. Compile the source into an executable, then run that executable. Compilation errors stop the program before execution.", "Missing a semicolon, mistyping main, or writing printf without including its declaration. A newline in output is written as \\n inside the string.", "Change the greeting, add a second printf, and explain which line executes first. Compile with -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic."},
    {"1. A declaration combines a type and a name: int count = 3.\n2. The type determines the operations and representation used. int stores whole numbers, while double is commonly used for fractional calculations.\n3. printf format specifiers must match argument types: %d for int, %f for double and %c for char.\n4. Use const when a variable should not be modified after initialization.", "Reading an uninitialized automatic variable gives an indeterminate value. Using %d with a double or assuming a particular int size is unsafe. Integer division discards the fractional part.", "Declare three variables for a student's marks, calculate their average using double, and print the result to two decimal places."},
    {"1. if selects a branch based on a condition. Use == for comparison and = for assignment.\n2. A for loop runs initialization once, checks the condition before each iteration, then performs its update.\n3. A while loop repeats while its condition stays true.\n4. Nested loops can build each row and column of a printed pattern.", "An accidental semicolon after for can create an empty loop body. Check off-by-one errors: i < n visits indexes 0 through n-1, but i <= n also attempts n.", "Print a triangle with four rows, then change the number of rows to six. Write a prime-number check using a loop."},
    {"1. A function declaration describes its return type, name and parameter types.\n2. Calling a C function passes argument values into parameters; changing the parameter alone does not change the caller's object.\n3. Return communicates a result, while pointer parameters can be used for output values.\n4. Keep functions focused and test boundary cases.", "Forgetting to return a value from a non-void function, confusing a local parameter with the caller's variable, or assuming a function can access variables from another scope.", "Implement isLeapYear(year), reverse the digits of a positive integer, and test isAPrimeNumber with 0, 1, 2 and 9."},
    {"1. An array stores elements consecutively; its first element has index zero.\n2. Pass an element count when passing an array to a function, because the parameter generally cannot recover its length.\n3. A char string must include a terminating zero byte.\n4. When copying strings, reserve enough room for content plus the terminator.", "An index equal to the element count is out of bounds. Forgetting the zero terminator lets string functions read beyond valid memory. Arrays passed to functions do not carry bounds automatically.", "Write find, reverse, rotateLeft and frequencyCount from Lab 1. Verify empty arrays and arrays with repeated elements."},
    {"1. A two-dimensional array uses row and column subscripts.\n2. For int grid[4][6], the memory layout contains four rows of six ints each.\n3. Iterate with a row loop outside a column loop.\n4. In a function parameter, the column dimension must be known for correct pointer arithmetic.", "Swapping row and column limits can access invalid cells. When checking a three-cell run, first ensure that two further positions remain in that direction.", "Implement a scan that finds the first horizontal or vertical run of three identical nonzero entries in a 4 x 6 grid."},
    {"1. A pointer contains an address, not the value at that address.\n2. &x produces the address of x; *p accesses the object pointed at by p when p is valid.\n3. After int *p = &x, assigning *p = 10 changes x itself.\n4. Initialize pointers and keep track of the lifetime of the pointed-to object.", "Confusing * in a declaration with * in an expression, dereferencing NULL, or using an uninitialized pointer. A pointer does not automatically allocate its target.", "Draw a memory diagram for int x=5; int *p=&x;. Predict the result of *p=8 and explain the relationship between p, &x and x."},
    {"1. A function receives a copy of a pointer value, which still identifies the caller's object.\n2. swap_ints uses a temporary to avoid losing one of the values.\n3. The caller passes addresses using &x and &y.\n4. Before dereferencing externally supplied pointers, define whether NULL is allowed.", "Writing a = b swaps local pointer variables, not the integers they point to. Writing *a = *b without a temporary loses the original value of *a.", "Implement swap_chars as well as swap_ints. Test with two distinct variables and verify both changed."},
    {"1. Pointer arithmetic is measured in elements of the pointed-to type.\n2. For a pointer to the first element, *(arr+i) means the element at index i.\n3. A pointer may point one past an array's end for comparison, but that one-past pointer must not be dereferenced.\n4. When iterating, advance while p < arr+size.", "Adding one to an int* does not mean moving one byte. Comparing pointers from unrelated arrays or dereferencing a pointer beyond the array is not valid array traversal.", "Implement find_max, sum_array and reverse_array from the pointer lab using only pointer arithmetic, never arr[i]."},
    {"1. A C string is a sequence of characters ending with '\\0'.\n2. To find length, move a pointer forward until the terminator is reached.\n3. To copy, copy the terminator as well as the visible characters.\n4. Lexicographic comparison must distinguish the first differing unsigned character values, or detect a terminator.", "Forgetting destination capacity, failing to copy the final zero byte, and treating uppercase and lowercase characters as equivalent without explicitly requesting that behavior.", "Implement strlen_ptr, count_vowels, copy_string, concat_strings and compare_strings, following the pointer-only lab restriction."},
    {"1. An automatic local object's lifetime normally ends when its block exits.\n2. A pointer to that expired object becomes dangling; reading through it is undefined behavior.\n3. Dynamically allocated memory has a separately controlled lifetime.\n4. Distinguish a memory leak (lost allocation) from use-after-free (using storage after release).", "Returning &local from a function is invalid once that function returns. Deleting memory does not automatically reset other pointers referring to it.", "Identify the lifetime bug in a function returning &x where x is local; rewrite it to return an int by value or use safe caller-provided storage."},
    {"1. In C, malloc obtains uninitialized storage; free releases that allocation.\n2. Check allocation results and calculate array sizes with sizeof *pointer.\n3. In C++, new constructs objects and delete destroys/deallocates them; array allocations use new[] and delete[].\n4. Modern C++ usually favors vector and smart pointers for ownership.", "Do not pair malloc with delete or new with free. Do not call free twice, use storage after free, or forget to free it on error paths.", "Allocate an integer array in C, initialize it, print its sum and free it. Sketch the equivalent C++ approach using std::vector."},
    {"1. A node groups a value and a pointer to the following node.\n2. A list head identifies the first node, and a null head represents an empty list.\n3. Traversal follows next pointers rather than computing an array index.\n4. Heap-allocated nodes need a clear owner responsible for releasing them.", "A node's next pointer must be initialized. Following an uninitialized next pointer may crash; never assume nodes are adjacent in memory.", "Create three nodes, link them in order, then draw each data field and arrow. Traverse until the next pointer is NULL."},
    {"1. Allocate or prepare a new node.\n2. Set newNode->next to the current head before replacing the head.\n3. Assign head = newNode (or *head = newNode when using Node **).\n4. This insertion works even if the original list was empty.", "Updating head before linking the new node loses access to the old list. Passing Node *head by value to a function cannot update the caller's head pointer.", "Write push_front, call it with an empty head, then add three values and verify they appear in reverse insertion order."},
    {"1. Construct a node whose next pointer is NULL.\n2. If head is NULL, the node becomes head.\n3. Otherwise, follow next until reaching the tail, and connect tail->next to the new node.\n4. If you maintain a tail pointer, append can be constant time.", "Dereferencing head->next when head is NULL crashes. If an allocated node is not inserted, its memory must still be released.", "Implement InsertAtEnd and test with an empty list, one existing node and several nodes. Compare traversal cost with tail-pointer insertion."},
    {"1. Traversal uses a temporary cursor, leaving the head pointer unchanged.\n2. For removal after a previous node, save the target and reconnect previous->next before freeing the target.\n3. Removing the first node requires updating head.\n4. After freeing a node, never access any of its fields.", "Freeing the target before saving its next pointer causes use-after-free. Also check the empty list, missing key and removal of the only node.", "Implement remove_first(head, value), print the list before and after, and run tests for head, middle, tail and absent values."},
    {"1. Each node has both prev and next links.\n2. To insert between A and B, set the new node's prev=A and next=B, then update A->next and B->prev.\n3. Empty-list and endpoint insertions require special handling.\n4. Traversal can run in either direction if head and tail are maintained.", "Forgetting one reciprocal link breaks reverse traversal. When deleting a node, update both neighbors before releasing its storage.", "Create DoublyLinkedNode and DoublyLinkedList and implement InsertAtBeginning, InsertAtEnd and InsertAtPosition as in the supplied lab."},
    {"1. A class groups state and operations; private fields hide implementation details.\n2. Constructors initialize instances and destructors can release resources.\n3. The public interface should expose only what callers need.\n4. Methods marked const promise not to modify the observable state of the object.", "The example uses C++ syntax and must be compiled with g++, not the C compiler that builds CodeQuest itself. Raw owning pointers need carefully defined copy and destruction behavior.", "Create a small Counter class with increment, reset and get, then write three tests for its public methods."},
    {"1. Templates express algorithms in terms of types rather than hard-coded int or float.\n2. A function template is instantiated for the type used at a call site.\n3. A class template can store and process values of a chosen type.\n4. The type must support every operation used in the template body.", "Template definitions generally need to be visible at their point of instantiation. A template that uses + cannot accept a type without a suitable + operation.", "Convert the lecture's sum function into a template and call it with int and double arrays. Explain what T represents."},
    {"1. Function overloading chooses between functions based on parameter types or counts.\n2. Operator overloading defines familiar operators for user-defined types.\n3. A member binary operator receives its left operand as *this, and its right operand as an argument.\n4. Prefix ++ and postfix ++ have different signatures and return conventions.", "You cannot overload on return type alone. Keep overloaded operators predictable; adding objects should have semantics users can understand.", "Write a Point operator+ and a prefix ++ for a counter-like class. Explain how the member signature differs from a non-member operator."},
    {"1. vector stores elements contiguously and can grow dynamically.\n2. push_back appends; size returns the element count.\n3. begin points to the first element and end is the past-the-end iterator.\n4. Growing a vector may reallocate storage and invalidate previous pointers or iterators.", "Do not dereference end(). Do not retain a reference to an element across operations that may reallocate the vector without confirming validity.", "Build a vector of movie lengths, loop over the elements using iterators, and find a pair whose sum equals a target flight duration."},
    {"1. std::list is commonly implemented as a doubly linked list.\n2. Its iterators can move forward and backward, but it does not offer numeric indexing.\n3. splice moves nodes between lists without copying element values.\n4. push_front, push_back and sort are list-specific operations explored in the lecture.", "Do not write myList[2] or add an integer to a list iterator. Standard-conforming splice keeps iterators and references to moved elements valid; their container changes.", "Create lists {1,2,3} and {10,20}, splice the second list into the first before 2, and inspect both lists afterward."},
    {"1. std::map maintains a sorted association between unique keys and values.\n2. find checks for a key without inserting a new one; operator[] may insert a default value.\n3. std::set contains unique keys, while stack and queue expose particular access patterns.\n4. Iterators allow traversal without exposing internal tree structure.", "Using const char* as a map key usually compares pointer addresses, not string contents. Prefer std::string when keys represent words.", "Build a word-frequency map<string,int>, print keys in sorted order and compare find with operator[] for a missing word."},
    {"1. Complexity describes how work grows with input size.\n2. A linear scan is O(n), while vector indexed access is O(1).\n3. Balanced tree map lookups are O(log n); list traversal is O(n).\n4. Standard algorithms are useful with matching iterator capabilities, such as std::sort with vector random-access iterators.", "Do not use std::sort directly on std::list iterators: list provides its own sort member. Think about iteration cost and invalidation rather than choosing a container by name alone.", "Compare searching 1,000 values using a vector scan, a sorted vector with binary search, and a map; describe the expected complexity."},
};
